\section{Levels and their order}{

The groups are the combinations of key values that occur in the rows of
the design, sorted as the levels of fixed effects are sorted (as
\code{xtx.fit::lm_xtx()} numbers them): by the first key variable, then the second,
and so on; numbers in increasing order, strings in the C locale (as
\code{sort(method = "radix")}: upper case before lower case), \code{FALSE} before
\code{TRUE}. Logical keys give integer levels \code{0} and \code{1}. Factors in memory
are sorted in the order of their levels; a remote data source has no
factors (strings, and DuckDB \code{ENUM} columns, are sorted as strings): for
the same groups on both backends, use character keys, or factors whose
levels are sorted.
}

\section{Missing keys}{

Every row of the design must have a group: a key with a missing value
(\code{NA} or \code{NaN} in memory, SQL \code{NULL} or \code{NaN} remotely) in a row of the
design is an error, on both backends. Fits with fixed effects drop such
rows; a grouping has no rows of its own, so select the rows first: for a
remote design, \code{D[DBI::SQL("k IS NOT NULL"), ]}; in memory, make the
design from the rows with a key. Missing values in the \emph{columns} are
kept: as \code{\link[=rowsum]{rowsum()}} does, \code{group_sums()} gives \code{NA} for a group with a
missing value, and \code{group_crosstab()} an \code{NA} count for a pair with a
missing weight.
}

\section{Rows, designs and backends}{

In memory, columns are plain vectors (or matrices) with one value per row
of the design, in the order of its rows. For a remote design, columns are
\code{\link[=design_column]{design_column()}}s of that design (or, where a column is expected, the
name of a column of the remote data or a \code{DBI::SQL()} expression): remote
rows have no order, so a vector in R cannot be matched with them, and
results with one value per row (\code{group_lookup()}) are columns. A
grouping combines only with columns and groupings of the design it was
made from (a row selection \code{D[i, ]} is another design; in memory, where
columns are plain vectors, only their length is checked); an in-memory
grouping does not combine with remote columns, nor the converse. Results
with one row per group (\code{group_sums()}, \code{group_crosstab()}) are in code
order, in R, without dimension names: row \code{l} is \code{g$levels[l, ]}. Both
backends give the same values, up to rounding of the sums.

A remote grouping is valid while its connection is open (see
\code{\link[=design_column]{design_column()}}, \emph{Lifetime}): \code{design_groups()} reads the levels (one
\verb{SELECT DISTINCT}) and writes them to a temporary table, and each use
joins that table to the rows, in the query that reads them.
}

